    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1645">

                <pb facs="1189" n="1073" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg1073"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 704.6712/3.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Morgenthau
                        to the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 199.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-01-30">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 30, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In acknowledgment of the Department’s
                    telegram No. 257 of the 10th [11th] instant, stating that American officers in
                    Mexico <pb facs="1190" n="1074" xml:id="pg1074"/> have been authorized to use
                    their good offices in that country for Turkish subjects, I have the honor to
                    state that this information has been communicated to the Sublime Porte. This was
                    done verbally, owing to the fact that the request which formed the subject of
                    the Embassy’s telegram of September 21 was a verbal one.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">A. Morgenthau.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
